{"source_id":"W2776643811","title":"An Investigation of Cognitive Processes Associated with Notetaking and Notes-Review","year":2017,"abstract":"Notetaking is a cognitively complex academic task that requires the execution of multiple cognitive processes within a limited capacity working memory (Peverly et al., 2007; Peverly & Sumowski, 2012; Piolat, Olive, & Kellogg, 2005). Several studies have examined the effects of notetaking and review on test performance, but few have examined the cognitive processes that underlie these effects. This dissertation examined the relation between transcription speed, working memory, and note quality with test performance in a sample of college students. Participants listened to a lecture, took notes, and were tested either immediately or after a delay with or without the opportunity to review their notes. Results indicated that note quality, but not modality of notetaking, predicted test performance, and that reviewing notes improved performance for all note takers regardless of modality."}
